SPANISH 3
&
\begin{verbatim}
%
%SUBGRAMMAR Identificational

(* DUTCH:

There are 6 rules, Ridentformation1-6.:

Ridentformation1: het/dit/dat is/zijn  de directeur/en\\
Ridentformation2: ik/jij/hij ben/bent/is het/dit/dat\\
Ridentformation3: wat is het/dit/dat\\
Ridentformation4: wat zijn /het/dit/dat\\
Ridentformation5: Hij is de directeur; Zij zijn de directeuren\\
Ridentformation6: De vraag is wat we doen; De stelling is dat we weggaan\\

In cases like  'wat zijn de problemen; wat is het probleem'
it is assumed that 'de problemen', 'het probleem' is the subject here?)\\

Cases such as Ik ben Ik; Ik ben jij  # not dealt with #

Translational problems:

{\em het } is de directeur \ \ \    {\em het } zijn de directeuren\\
\ \ \ {\em it } is the director \ \ \ \ \     {\em these } are the directors\\
\ \ \ {\em es } el director \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ son los directores\\

wat is het/dit/dat \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \  wat zijn het/dit/dat?\\
\ \ \ what is it/this/that \ \ \ \ \ \ \  what are these/these/those\\
\ \ \ qu\'{e} es/-/esto/eso \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ qu\'{e} son -/esos/estos\\

dit/dat ben ik \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ dit/dat zijn wij \\
\ \ \ it/This/That is me \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ it/this/that is us \\
\ \ \ soy yo \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ somos nosotros \\

cf. also (predicatively):

het zijn aardige jongens \\
\ \ \ these are nice guys \\
\ \ \ son  chicos amables \\

Ik ben jou en jij bent mij \\
\ \ \ I am you and you are me \\
\ \ \ ?Yo soy tu y tu eres yo \\

Agreement.\\
From such examples as {\em dit schijnen de directeuren te zijn} we must 
conclude that the verb {\em schijnen} agrees with {\em dit}, which in itself
agrees with the idrel NP, i.c. {\em de directeuren}.

We have decided to introduce {\em het, dit, dat} not by means of a variable,
because these words can be spelled out finally by totally different words
than the original ones (e.g. in English {\em it} plus a plural NP must become
e.g. {\em these}. It is perhaps possible to deal with this by means of
variable, but then the variable must change during the derivation. Would that 
be an acceptable option?, or must we translate {\em het} both as {\em it} and
as {\em these} (but if so, then can we exclude these elsewhere?)

In the subject-verb agreement rule the NPrecords over dit, dat, het must be
reset, to avoid superfluous ambiguities in the surface parser. the same must 
happen upon Case assignment to dit, dat, het in e.g. Ik vind dit het 
belangrijkste probleem. 

In contrast to the isomorphic scheme, the identificational subgrammar now
generates  NPPROPs. This is absolutely required for such sentences as 
{\em dit lijken de belangrijkste problemen }.

{\em Wat} is inherently only singular (cf. {\em *wat bewijzen dit?});\\
{\em Dat} is inherently only singular (cf. {\em *dat bewijzen iets });\\
In combination with the verb {\em zijn}, however, both {\em wat zijn dat} and
{\em wat is dat} are possible. For this purpose two rules, one for the
singular, and one for the plural, have been written.

In the last rule there is a dependency between the mood and the finiteness of
the sentence and the nounpattern of the noun heading the NP that will be 
substituted for the NPVAR. There is no check on this dependency in the
rule. It is also not easy to build such a restriction in, because the relevant
property is only present at the NOUN heading the NP that will be substituted
for the NPVAR, and this NOUN is not accessible in this rule. We might transport
the value of the relevant attribute via the NP and the pre-action to the NPVAR,
but then a special attribute for this purpose would be required. Furthermore,
it might be that the relevant dependency is most appropriately checked by the
type system, and not in the syntax, so that a sentence such as 
{\em De vraag is dat we weggaan}  is not syntactically, but semantically ill-
formed.

For Case assignment it must be known later, whether the NPPROP contains
an identificational or an predicational structure. This is recorded 
by the special relation idrel.\\
# Maybe this can be dropped #

The distinction between the identificational cases and the predicational cases
has been drawn in a rather arbitrary, and certainly not fully principled way.
Further investigation into a more principled partitioning (or perhaps no 
partitioning at all) is desirable. Many questions are still open; there are
many translational problems in this area. 

Why is {\em *Het ben ik} not wellformed? (in contrast to {\em dit/dat ben ik})\
\
Why :\\
*dat is mij/me;\\ 
*dat is jou/je;\\ 
dat is *hem/OK 'm;\\ 
dat is *haar/d'r\\
And Why:\\
dat lijk ik wel/dat lijkt mij wel; \\
dat lijk jij wel/dat lijkt jou wel; \\
dat lijkt hij wel; dat lijkt hem wel; \\
dat lijken wij wel; ?dat lijkt ons wel \\
dat lijken jullie wel; *dat lijkt jullie wel \\
dat lijken zij wel; ?dat lijkt hen wel \\

'het ben ik' is unwellformed because 'ik' is the subject, 'het' is a
complement, and 'het' can never be topicalized unless it is a subject. 

Cf. also

*Hij zegt dat dat ik ben   /.. dat ik dat ben \\
\ \ \ *Hij zegt dat het ik ben   /.. dat ik het ben \\

This is so if there is a (nominative?) pronoun in the NP, but not if there
is a full NP: \\ 
Hij  zegt dat het/dit/dat  de directeur is. \\

                                           (Jan Odijk) *)


  (* RC_StartIdentificational *)
( RidentformationESTO/1    | RidentformationESO1/2   | 
  RidentformationESO2/3    | RPersproIdent/4         | 
  RSentIdent/5             | RDemproWhIdentSg/6      |
  RDemproWhIdentPl/7 
)


.{ RIdentConjsentVar/10 | RIdentFinalConjsentVar/11 |
   RIdentLocAdvVar/12   | RIdentLocPreppVar/13      | Ridentsadvvar/14 | 
   Ridentspreppvar/15   | RIdentRefVarInsert/8 
 }


HEAD:
<
NPVAR         BASIC EXPRESSION
SENTENCEVAR   BASIC EXPRESSION
>

(* IMPORT: *)
<
 1: < ARGUMENT 2    NP            FROM (NPFormation)       
    >
 2: < ARGUMENT 2    NP            FROM (NPFormation)       
    >
 3: < ARGUMENT 2    NP            FROM (NPFormation)       
    >
 4: < ARGUMENT 2    NP            FROM (NPFormation)       
    >
 5: < ARGUMENT 2    NPVAR         BASIC EXPRESSION         
    >
 6: < ARGUMENT 2    NPVAR         BASIC EXPRESSION  
    >
 7: < ARGUMENT 2    PREPPVAR      BASIC EXPRESSION         
      ARGUMENT 2    ADVPVAR       BASIC EXPRESSION         
      ARGUMENT 2    SENTENCEVAR   BASIC EXPRESSION         
    >
 8: < ARGUMENT 2    ADVPVAR       BASIC EXPRESSION         
    >
 9: < ARGUMENT 2    PREPPVAR      BASIC EXPRESSION         
    >
10: < ARGUMENT 2    ADVPVAR       BASIC EXPRESSION         
      ARGUMENT 2    PREPPVAR      BASIC EXPRESSION         
    >
11: < ARGUMENT 2    SENTENCEVAR   BASIC EXPRESSION         
      ARGUMENT 2    PREPPVAR      BASIC EXPRESSION         
    >
12: < ARGUMENT 2    SENTENCEVAR   BASIC EXPRESSION         
      ARGUMENT 2    PREPPVAR      BASIC EXPRESSION         
    >
>

EXPORT:
<
NPPROP
>

&
\end{verbatim}


\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% RULE RidentformationESTO
< m1: I2::NPVAR{NPVARrec1}
  m2: NP{NPrec1}[mu1] 
>

< m :NPPROP{NPPROPrec1}
           [ subjrel/NP{NPrec2}[mu2],
             idrel/I2::NPVAR{NPVARrec1} 
           ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I2: NOT (NPVARrec1.NPhead IN [quewhproNP, persproNP])
      >


<
   SUBRULE (* 1. éste *)
      < mu1: [head/DEMPRO(KEY_estoDEMPROkey){DEMPROrec1}] >
      < mu2: [head/DEMPRO(KEY_esteDEMPROkey){DEMPROrec2}] >

      COMP
         <
          C1: NPrec1 = AUX_NPONestoDMPR EXCEPT FOR
                         [supertiempo, generic]  AND
              NPrec1.supertiempo = presente      AND
              NPrec1.generic = nogeneric         AND
              NPVARrec1.numero = singular        AND
              NPVARrec1.genero = masc
          A1: NPrec2 := AUX_NPONesteDMPR;
              NPrec2.supertiempo := omegatiempo;
              NPrec2.generic := omegageneric;
              NPPROPrec1.aktionsarts := [stative]
              (* rest of npprop remains default *)
         >


   SUBRULE (* 2. ésta *)
      < mu1: [head/DEMPRO(KEY_estoDEMPROkey){DEMPROrec1}] >
      < mu2: [head/DEMPRO(KEY_estaDEMPROkey){DEMPROrec2}] >

      COMP
         <
          C1: NPrec1 = AUX_NPONestoDMPR EXCEPT FOR
                         [supertiempo, generic]  AND
              NPrec1.supertiempo = presente      AND
              NPrec1.generic = nogeneric         AND
              NPVARrec1.numero = singular        AND
              NPVARrec1.genero = fem
          A1: NPrec2 := AUX_NPONestaDMPR;
              NPrec2.supertiempo := omegatiempo;
              NPrec2.generic := omegageneric;
              NPPROPrec1.aktionsarts := [stative]
              (* rest of npprop remains default *)
         >

   SUBRULE (* 3. éstos *)
      < mu1: [head/DEMPRO(KEY_estoDEMPROkey){DEMPROrec1}] >
      < mu2: [head/DEMPRO(KEY_estosDEMPROkey){DEMPROrec2}] >

      COMP
         <
          C1: NPrec1 = AUX_NPONestoDMPR EXCEPT FOR
                         [supertiempo, generic]  AND
              NPrec1.supertiempo = presente      AND
              NPrec1.generic = nogeneric         AND
              NPVARrec1.numero = plural          AND
              NPVARrec1.genero = masc
          A1: NPrec2 := AUX_NPONestosDMPR;
              NPrec2.supertiempo := omegatiempo;
              NPrec2.generic := omegageneric;
              NPPROPrec1.aktionsarts := [stative]
              (* rest of npprop remains default *)
         >

   SUBRULE (* 4. éstas *)
      < mu1: [head/DEMPRO(KEY_estoDEMPROkey){DEMPROrec1}] >
      < mu2: [head/DEMPRO(KEY_estasDEMPROkey){DEMPROrec2}] >

      COMP
         <
          C1: NPrec1 = AUX_NPONestoDMPR EXCEPT FOR
                         [supertiempo, generic]  AND
              NPrec1.supertiempo = presente      AND
              NPrec1.generic = nogeneric         AND
              NPVARrec1.numero = plural          AND
              NPVARrec1.genero = fem
          A1: NPrec2 := AUX_NPONestasDMPR;
              NPrec2.supertiempo := omegatiempo;
              NPrec2.generic := omegageneric;
              NPPROPrec1.aktionsarts := [stative]
              (* rest of npprop remains default *)
         >


   SUBRULE (* 5. redelloNP *)
      < mu1: [head/PERSPRO{PERSPROrec1}
                  [head/BPERSPRO(KEY_elloBPERSPROkey){BPERSPROrec1}]
             ] 
      >
      < mu2: [head/PERSPRO{PERSPROrec2}
                  [head/BPERSPRO(KEY_redelloBPERSPROkey){BPERSPROrec2}]
             ] 
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: NPrec1.supertiempo = presente AND
              NPrec1.generic = nogeneric    AND
              PERSPROrec1 = COPYT_bTOperspro(BPERSPROrec1) AND
              NPVARrec1.numero = singular 

          A1: PERSPROrec2 := COPYT_bTOperspro(BPERSPROrec2);
              PERSPROrec2.forma := nomdebil;
              NPrec2 := AUX_NPONredelloBPRSPR;
              NPrec2.supertiempo := omegatiempo;
              NPrec2.generic := omegageneric;
              NPPROPrec1.aktionsarts := [stative]
              (* rest of npprop remains default *)
         > 

   SUBRULE (* 6. redellosNP *)
      < mu1: [head/PERSPRO{PERSPROrec1}
                  [head/BPERSPRO(KEY_elloBPERSPROkey){BPERSPROrec1}]
             ] 
      >
      < mu2: [head/PERSPRO{PERSPROrec2}
                  [head/BPERSPRO(KEY_redellosBPERSPROkey){BPERSPROrec2}]
             ] 
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: NPrec1.supertiempo = presente AND
              NPrec1.generic = nogeneric    AND
              PERSPROrec1 = COPYT_bTOperspro(BPERSPROrec1) AND
              NPVARrec1.numero = plural

          A1: PERSPROrec2 := COPYT_bTOperspro(BPERSPROrec2);
              NPrec2 := AUX_NPONnomBPRSPR;                     
              NPrec2.numero := plural;                     
              NPrec2.persona := 3;
              NPrec2.genero := masc;
              NPrec1.NPhead := redellosNP;
              NPrec2.supertiempo := omegatiempo;
              NPrec2.generic := omegageneric;
              NPPROPrec1.aktionsarts := [stative]
              (* rest of npprop remains default *)
         > 
>
&

\end{verbatim}
